Today, I want to talk about a problem
that becomes increasingly important as a

3
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:14,710
generative AI moves from experimentation
into a real production system.

4
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:19,579
The problem is we have become
very good at measuring models.

5
00:00:20,419 --> 00:00:25,240
We have benchmarks, we have leaderboards,
we have standardized datasets.

6
00:00:25,749 --> 00:00:31,280
We have a impressive model comparison,
but when we take those same models

7
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:36,190
and put them into a production,
the behavior can be very different.

8
00:00:37,019 --> 00:00:42,440
A model can perform extremely well
on a benchmark and still produces

9
00:00:42,929 --> 00:00:45,049
unreliable answers in production.

10
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:53,100
It can handle the ha-- it can hallucinate,
it can fail on unexpected user input,

11
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:58,600
it can retrieve a wrong content it can
give technically reasonable answer that

12
00:00:58,609 --> 00:01:01,350
doesn't actually solve the user problem.

13
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:06,100
And sometimes the model
itself isn't even the problem.

14
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:11,080
The failure can come from retrieval,
prompting, orchestration, integration, or

15
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:15,140
the way the overall system is designed.

16
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:23,330
So today I want to explore how we
most simplify evaluating a model to

17
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:25,720
evaluating the complete AI system.

18
00:01:27,210 --> 00:01:31,440
This is basically we are trying to
move from simply evaluating a model

19
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:37,280
to e-evaluating complete AI system
continuously, measurably, and in a way

20
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:43,780
ultimately connects to the business impact
about me before I get into the topic

