I have my master's from VIT, Vellore.

30
00:02:26,370 --> 00:02:27,920
So moving on.

31
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:34,570
I have much of my expertise with
Java, Python, cloud platform AI, ML

32
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:36,480
frameworks, DevOps, and observability.

33
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:42,800
So for this session, I want to start with
why traditional benchmarks fall short.

34
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:45,230
Let's start with the fundamental problem.

35
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:47,790
Traditional benchmarks are useful.

36
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:50,770
I'm not suggesting that
we throw away them.

37
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:56,590
They are very valuable for comparing model
capabilities under a controlled condition.

38
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:01,350
But production AI is completely
a different environment.

39
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,770
A benchmark typically gives us a
stat a static curated data set.

40
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:09,220
A benchmark is typically
a cur-curated data set.

41
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:13,420
Production gives us dynamic
and unpredictable user inputs.

42
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:17,570
A benchmark might evaluate
on a single transaction.

43
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:22,780
A production system often involves
multi-turn conversation where

44
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:24,930
context accumulates over time.

45
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:29,160
Benchmark is usually has a
clearly defined objective.

46
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:33,020
Production systems have a
business-specific objective that

47
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,000
can be much more complicated.

48
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:41,060
And perhaps most importantly, benchmarks
tends to isolate to isolate the model.

49
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:42,790
Production system too.

50
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,860
Production has a retrieval system.

51
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:47,580
It has prompt templates.

52
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:48,270
It has orchestration.

53
00:03:48,930 --> 00:03:50,000
It has APIs.

54
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:51,010
It has databases.

55
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:52,310
It has permissions.

56
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,040
It has safety layers and it has
the latency constraints, and

57
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:02,410
it has a real user doing things
that you never anticipated when

58
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:03,920
you designed the benchmark.

59
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:09,420
That's why a model can score extremely
well in a controlled environment

60
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:13,580
and still behaves unpredictably
when exposed to a real world.

61
00:04:14,230 --> 00:04:15,910
Think about it, it in this way.

62
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,960
A benchmark is asking
how capable is the model?

63
00:04:20,570 --> 00:04:25,180
Production evaluation needs to ask
how does the entire system behaves

64
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:29,860
when a re-real user depend on it?

65
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:35,300
That is much harder question,
but it is also a-- the

66
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:36,560
question that actually matters

67
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:40,160
From model evaluation to
the system evaluation.

68
00:04:40,170 --> 00:04:44,230
So this leads to the first
major shift in our thinking.

69
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:48,360
Instead of asking, is the model
responsible, we need to ask,

70
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:54,090
does the complete AI system
consistently deliver reliable, safe,

71
00:04:54,510 --> 00:04:56,150
relevant, and valuable outcome?

72
00:04:56,740 --> 00:05:02,080
That's fundamentally the different
definition of evaluation, and I would

73
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:04,480
break that into four activities.

74
00:05:04,510 --> 00:05:06,080
First one is the measure.

75
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:12,190
We need to define what quality
actually mean for our application,

76
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:16,520
and then translate that definition
into a measurable signal.

77
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:18,470
Second is the validate.

78
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:24,370
We need to automate evaluation,
but we also need to need human

79
00:05:24,370 --> 00:05:28,300
judgment where automation--
automated systems aren't sufficient.

80
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:30,800
Third is to monitor.

81
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:36,190
We can't evaluate a system once before
launch and then forget about it.

82
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:42,000
We need to continuously detect
degradation, drift, and new failure modes.

83
00:05:43,070 --> 00:05:45,180
Fourth, and finally, the important govern.

84
00:05:45,870 --> 00:05:50,040
We need traceability, we need
accountability, we need controls

85
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:55,960
that allows us to understand what
happened, why it happened, and what

86
00:05:55,960 --> 00:06:00,640
can we do-- what we can do about it.

87
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:06,420
So the evaluation system itself becomes
part of production architecture.

88
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:09,830
That's the transition I'm advocating here.

89
00:06:10,830 --> 00:06:17,310
From model evaluation to a system
evaluation, and from a one-time

90
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:21,730
test to a continuous engineering
discipline What should we evaluate?

91
00:06:22,730 --> 00:06:26,110
So once we make that shift,
the next question is, what

92
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:27,610
exactly should we measure?

93
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:31,790
I think there are four dimensions
that provides a strong foundation.

94
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:33,740
The first is the factual accuracy.

95
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,740
Is the information factually correct?

96
00:06:36,790 --> 00:06:39,220
For example, we are seeing hallucinations.

97
00:06:39,930 --> 00:06:41,430
Are citations accurate?

98
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:44,530
Does the output match our ground truth?

99
00:06:45,190 --> 00:06:46,570
The second is grounding.

100
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:51,030
Even if an answer sounds
correct, is it actually supported

101
00:06:51,030 --> 00:06:52,510
by authoritative content?

102
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:58,110
This is specifically important
for retrieval augmented

103
00:06:58,130 --> 00:06:59,690
systems, like the RAG system.

104
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:05,154
We want to know whether the retrieved
information was relevant, whether the

105
00:07:05,154 --> 00:07:10,880
answer was faithful to that information,
and whether the system can attribute

106
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:12,550
its claim to the right source.

107
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,820
The third dimension is the safety.

108
00:07:14,830 --> 00:07:19,190
Does the system behave within the
policy boundaries we have defined?

109
00:07:19,490 --> 00:07:23,210
And finally, the fourth
is the task completion.

110
00:07:23,790 --> 00:07:26,810
This is-- This one is easy to understand.

111
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:31,040
A response can be factually
correct and still fail the user.

112
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:37,670
Suppose the user can- came to accomplish
a particular task, did the system

113
00:07:37,670 --> 00:07:39,400
actually help them to complete it?

114
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:45,180
That's why task su-success rate and
downstream outcomes are more important.

115
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,900
And this gives us an important principle.

116
00:07:50,490 --> 00:07:56,280
The best AI system is not necessarily
the one with the highest model score.

117
00:07:56,640 --> 00:08:02,650
It is one the-- that reliably
satisfies the objective that matters

118
00:08:02,650 --> 00:08:05,360
for the application Moving on.

119
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:08,560
Make AI quality observable.

120
00:08:09,750 --> 00:08:15,290
Now, measuring quality is one-- is only
useful if we can actually observe it.

121
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:20,660
This is where observability becomes
a critical part of AI engineering.

122
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:26,410
For a production AI application,
we want to measure signals such as

123
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:33,410
hallucination rate, response relevance,
user feedback and tasks task success.

124
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:38,210
But the important word
here is the continuous.

125
00:08:39,170 --> 00:08:44,020
The-- These metrics cannot simply be
calculated once before the launch.

126
00:08:44,050 --> 00:08:47,090
They need to be tracked
across production interaction.

127
00:08:47,540 --> 00:08:51,550
Think about an application that
performs well when you first release it.

128
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:56,060
The model may change, the prompt
may change, the retrieval system may

129
00:08:56,060 --> 00:09:01,060
change, the underlying data may change,
and the u-user behavior may change.

130
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:05,250
Any of those things can alter
the quality of the system.

131
00:09:05,510 --> 00:09:11,480
So we need to treat AI quality the same
way we treat other production signals.

132
00:09:11,950 --> 00:09:17,180
We observe it, we measure it, we look for
the degradation, we segment it, and we

133
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:22,770
investigate when something moves into a
wrong direction-- in the wrong direction.

134
00:09:23,050 --> 00:09:29,650
But there are-- Another important point,
not every metric is equally valuable.

135
00:09:29,870 --> 00:09:34,260
A metric should connect to
the application's risk profile

136
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:36,090
and business objectives.

137
00:09:36,630 --> 00:09:40,090
Because at the end of the day, we
are not collecting metrics just

138
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:42,360
because metrics are interesting.

139
00:09:42,710 --> 00:09:45,810
We are collecting them so
that we can make a better

140
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:47,980
engineering and product decisions.

141
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:52,020
Evaluation must be a domain-specific.

142
00:09:53,010 --> 00:09:56,310
This brings us to another
critical principle.

143
00:09:56,410 --> 00:10:02,230
There is no universal evaluation
framework that works equally

144
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:04,880
well for every AI application.

145
00:10:05,370 --> 00:10:07,840
The evaluation has to be domain-specific.

146
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:09,580
Consider customer support.

147
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:15,240
The, the task completion and resolution
rate might be extremely important.

148
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:22,500
You might care about whether the
user got an issue, got a issue

149
00:10:22,530 --> 00:10:24,510
resolved without escalation.

150
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:27,450
Now compare that with a
decision support system.

151
00:10:29,010 --> 00:10:33,110
In that environment, evidence,
traceability and confidence

152
00:10:33,110 --> 00:10:35,520
calibration may matter much more.

153
00:10:35,970 --> 00:10:40,750
The question isn't just did the
system give you, give an answer?

154
00:10:41,210 --> 00:10:44,290
The question is, can I understand
the evidence behind that

155
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:46,440
answer, and should I trust it?

156
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:54,742
Those fundamentally different
requirements And that's why simply

157
00:10:55,022 --> 00:10:59,902
taking a generic benchmark and
applying it to every AI application

158
00:10:59,902 --> 00:11:01,892
can produce misleading results.

159
00:11:02,812 --> 00:11:07,522
You might get a great score and
still be measuring the wrong things.

160
00:11:07,532 --> 00:11:12,072
So evaluation rubric should
start with this, with the domain.

161
00:11:12,682 --> 00:11:16,072
What does the quality mean
in a particular application?

162
00:11:16,442 --> 00:11:18,392
What does failure look like?

163
00:11:19,482 --> 00:11:21,632
What is the cost of that failure?

164
00:11:22,432 --> 00:11:26,132
And what outcomes are we
ultimately trying to improve?

165
00:11:27,032 --> 00:11:31,072
Once we answer those questions,
we can define the metrics.

166
00:11:32,072 --> 00:11:36,432
In other words, first define
what matters, then measure it

167
00:11:37,432 --> 00:11:37,802
Moving on.

168
00:11:38,562 --> 00:11:42,652
Automated evaluations
plus human-in-the-loop.

169
00:11:43,502 --> 00:11:49,132
At production scale, we obviously
cannot have humans manually

170
00:11:49,172 --> 00:11:51,052
review every single interaction.

171
00:11:51,652 --> 00:11:54,072
That would be too expensive and too slow.

172
00:11:54,552 --> 00:12:00,232
But completely automated evaluations
also has limitation, and that's why

173
00:12:00,262 --> 00:12:06,312
I believe the right approach is scale
with automation, validate with humans.

174
00:12:07,182 --> 00:12:09,012
Automation gives us a scale.

175
00:12:10,392 --> 00:12:15,842
We can use the techniques such as LLM
as a judge, embedding similarities,

176
00:12:15,912 --> 00:12:21,712
classifiers, rule-based checks,
factuality checks, and automated safety

177
00:12:21,712 --> 00:12:26,722
checks that allow us to evaluate a
very large number of interaction.

178
00:12:27,212 --> 00:12:30,462
But we shouldn't treat
all interactions equally.

179
00:12:31,012 --> 00:12:34,312
Instead, we should use
intelligent sampling.

180
00:12:35,332 --> 00:12:38,392
For example, we can prioritize
low-confidence inter--

181
00:12:38,452 --> 00:12:44,352
interaction, high-risk interaction,
policy-sensitive interactions, and

182
00:12:44,362 --> 00:12:47,532
statistically significant edge cases.

183
00:12:47,952 --> 00:12:51,942
Those are the interactions where
human expertise has the highest

184
00:12:52,022 --> 00:12:56,832
value Then the human reviewer
becomes the validation layer of--

185
00:12:57,092 --> 00:13:00,562
for ambiguous or complex s-scenarios.

186
00:13:01,232 --> 00:13:04,502
So we are not choosing
between automation and humans.

187
00:13:04,542 --> 00:13:10,032
We are designing a system where each
is used for what it does the best.

188
00:13:10,882 --> 00:13:13,022
Automation handles breadth and scale.

189
00:13:13,382 --> 00:13:16,622
Human provides judgment where
the problem is difficult,

190
00:13:16,692 --> 00:13:18,452
ambiguous, or high consequence.

191
00:13:20,422 --> 00:13:23,902
That combination is much more
scalable than manual review

192
00:13:24,182 --> 00:13:29,832
alone and much more trustworthy
than simulation automation alone

193
00:13:30,832 --> 00:13:31,382
Moving on.

194
00:13:31,392 --> 00:13:32,172
Building

195
00:13:33,172 --> 00:13:34,622
A continuous quality loop.

196
00:13:36,112 --> 00:13:39,962
And this leads naturally to
the next concept, evaluation

197
00:13:40,372 --> 00:13:41,802
should not end with a score.

198
00:13:42,652 --> 00:13:44,932
A score by itself doesn't
improve the system.

199
00:13:45,522 --> 00:13:49,772
The real value comes when the
evaluation creates a feedback loop.

200
00:13:50,552 --> 00:13:54,772
Imagine a production interaction
goes through an automated evaluation.

201
00:13:56,322 --> 00:13:59,292
Some of those interactions
are identified as risky.

202
00:13:59,692 --> 00:14:03,332
A subset is sent to a human reviewers.

203
00:14:03,892 --> 00:14:07,322
The human reviewers identify
a ba-- failure pattern.

204
00:14:07,802 --> 00:14:09,002
Now what happened?

205
00:14:09,522 --> 00:14:12,912
That information can improve
the evaluation model.

206
00:14:13,492 --> 00:14:15,642
It can improve the scoring rubric.

207
00:14:15,872 --> 00:14:18,942
It can improve the ground true data set.

208
00:14:19,872 --> 00:14:24,372
It can improve the annotation
guidelines, and it can ultimately

209
00:14:24,392 --> 00:14:26,122
improve the AI system itself.

210
00:14:27,052 --> 00:14:30,142
This is what I mean by
continuous quality loop.

211
00:14:30,912 --> 00:14:34,342
Traditional evaluation
often works like a gate.

212
00:14:34,842 --> 00:14:38,652
You test the system, you decide
whether it passes, then you deploy.

213
00:14:39,182 --> 00:14:44,262
But a production AI system doesn't
stop changing after deployment, so

214
00:14:44,302 --> 00:14:46,472
evaluation has to evolve with it.

215
00:14:46,642 --> 00:14:52,232
Every meaningful production failure should
be viewed as more than just a defect.

216
00:14:52,622 --> 00:14:55,132
It should be treated as an information.

217
00:14:55,432 --> 00:15:00,982
It tell us, it tells us something
about the AI system, and sometimes

218
00:15:00,992 --> 00:15:05,302
it tells us something about our
evaluation methodology as well.

219
00:15:06,272 --> 00:15:08,902
Maybe our benchmark wasn't representative.

220
00:15:08,902 --> 00:15:12,302
Maybe our rubric missed
an important dimension.

221
00:15:12,632 --> 00:15:15,752
Maybe our judge prompt wasn't good enough.

222
00:15:16,232 --> 00:15:18,862
Maybe our ground true data set is stale.

223
00:15:19,512 --> 00:15:25,212
That is how evaluations become a living
system rather than one-time gate.

224
00:15:26,212 --> 00:15:29,152
Hallucination detect
before it reaches the user.

225
00:15:30,212 --> 00:15:35,942
Let's take one of the biggest
problem in production environments.

226
00:15:36,942 --> 00:15:42,262
Hallucination is particularly dangerous
because a response can sound extremely

227
00:15:42,322 --> 00:15:44,812
convincing while still being wrong.

228
00:15:46,212 --> 00:15:49,782
And there is no single technique
that catches every hallucination,

229
00:15:50,212 --> 00:15:52,572
so we need a layered approach.

230
00:15:52,882 --> 00:15:55,772
One layer is entitlement-based checking

231
00:15:56,772 --> 00:15:58,482
Entitlement-based checking.

232
00:15:59,592 --> 00:16:03,782
We ask whether the claims in the
answer are actually supported by

233
00:16:03,852 --> 00:16:06,212
authoritative retrieved context.

234
00:16:06,702 --> 00:16:10,112
Another technique is
self-consistency sampling.

235
00:16:10,652 --> 00:16:15,262
We compare multiple generations and
look for the stability or contradiction.

236
00:16:16,062 --> 00:16:18,272
Then we have retrieval faithfulness.

237
00:16:18,432 --> 00:16:23,152
Here we are asking whether the claims in
the final response are actually supported

238
00:16:23,152 --> 00:16:26,062
by the evidence that was retrieved.

239
00:16:26,772 --> 00:16:30,182
And finally, we can introduce
inference time controls.

240
00:16:31,642 --> 00:16:37,132
These might include confidence
thresholds, constraint generation,

241
00:16:37,362 --> 00:16:38,902
or human escalation paths.

242
00:16:40,102 --> 00:16:44,142
The important point is that
detection is not the ultimate goal.

243
00:16:44,152 --> 00:16:45,912
The goal is the prevention.

244
00:16:46,472 --> 00:16:51,702
We don't want to discover that
a response was unreliable after

245
00:16:51,742 --> 00:16:53,582
it has already reached the user.

246
00:16:54,222 --> 00:17:00,532
We want to build enough controls
into our pipeline that unreliable

247
00:17:00,582 --> 00:17:05,252
responses are intercepted before
they become production incidents.

248
00:17:05,562 --> 00:17:10,922
So hallucination detection system
becomes not just an analytical problem,

249
00:17:11,372 --> 00:17:13,982
but an engineering control problem

250
00:17:14,982 --> 00:17:19,202
Robustness, bias, and adversary input.

251
00:17:19,202 --> 00:17:21,872
Another reason traditional
benchmarks fall short is that

252
00:17:21,892 --> 00:17:24,182
real users are unpredictable.

253
00:17:25,062 --> 00:17:33,102
The input we receive in production are
often very different from the carefully

254
00:17:33,532 --> 00:17:35,562
curated inputs in the evaluation data set.

255
00:17:36,772 --> 00:17:44,262
User paraphrases questions, they make
typos, they provide incomplete context,

256
00:17:44,292 --> 00:17:50,382
they combine multiple intent, they create
ambiguous requests, and sometimes they

257
00:17:50,382 --> 00:17:52,702
intentionally try to break the system.

258
00:17:53,292 --> 00:17:56,612
So the production evaluation
needs to cover these scenarios.

259
00:17:57,322 --> 00:18:00,892
One technique is input perturbation.

260
00:18:01,462 --> 00:18:07,382
We deliberately test noisy, ambiguous,
paraphrased, and adversarial questions.

261
00:18:08,212 --> 00:18:11,332
We also need to consider
fairness evaluation.

262
00:18:11,652 --> 00:18:15,792
Are we seeing systematic differences
in the response quality or

263
00:18:15,802 --> 00:18:17,852
safety across relevant scenarios?

264
00:18:18,722 --> 00:18:20,882
Then we have red team testing.

265
00:18:21,032 --> 00:18:26,452
We proactively look for jailbreaking
vulnerabilities, prompt injection

266
00:18:26,452 --> 00:18:28,832
weaknesses, and policy edge cases.

267
00:18:29,552 --> 00:18:32,692
And finally, there is a
distribution shift monitoring.

268
00:18:33,142 --> 00:18:38,252
The user population and the input
distribution can evolve over time.

269
00:18:39,632 --> 00:18:43,472
So if the characteristics of the
production traffic meaningfully changes,

270
00:18:43,902 --> 00:18:46,772
that should trigger a re-evaluation.

271
00:18:47,352 --> 00:18:50,182
This is an important mindset change.

272
00:18:50,432 --> 00:18:53,682
Robustness isn't something we prove once.

273
00:18:53,862 --> 00:18:57,061
It is something we continuously
test as the environment

274
00:18:57,702 --> 00:19:00,392
evolves or environment changes

275
00:19:01,392 --> 00:19:04,862
Continuous evaluation
across the AI lifecycle.

276
00:19:05,472 --> 00:19:11,142
This is one of the most important phase
important thing to have in our production

277
00:19:11,142 --> 00:19:15,622
environment, and that's why evaluation
cannot be limited to pre-production.

278
00:19:16,132 --> 00:19:20,182
We need evaluation across
the entire AI lifecycle.

279
00:19:20,182 --> 00:19:24,082
We start with the model selection,
then we move into development,

280
00:19:24,262 --> 00:19:26,472
then validation, then deployment.

281
00:19:27,002 --> 00:19:33,452
And after deployment, the process
continues because production can reveal

282
00:19:33,522 --> 00:19:35,882
things that weren't visible earlier.

283
00:19:36,552 --> 00:19:39,122
Maybe the model starts
degrading over time.

284
00:19:39,622 --> 00:19:42,052
Maybe new failure modes emerge.

285
00:19:42,462 --> 00:19:44,592
Maybe users' behavior change.

286
00:19:46,442 --> 00:19:48,492
Maybe the input distribution shifts.

287
00:19:48,932 --> 00:19:51,482
Maybe emerging safety risk appears.

288
00:19:51,802 --> 00:19:56,592
Those are all reasons
to continue evaluating.

289
00:19:57,252 --> 00:20:03,372
One of the biggest mistake we can make
is to think of evaluations as a gate

290
00:20:03,882 --> 00:20:07,242
that exists immediately before launch.

291
00:20:07,882 --> 00:20:10,532
That creates a dangerous blind spot.

292
00:20:10,892 --> 00:20:13,332
The system can change after deployment.

293
00:20:13,892 --> 00:20:15,522
The environment changes.

294
00:20:16,282 --> 00:20:21,162
The user can change, and the risk change.

295
00:20:21,792 --> 00:20:25,002
So our evaluation system needs
to change along with them.

296
00:20:25,522 --> 00:20:34,462
A useful way to think about this is
a-- an AI system evolves continuously,

297
00:20:34,562 --> 00:20:40,102
and the evaluation system that govern
them must evolve continuously as well

298
00:20:41,102 --> 00:20:43,412
Production evaluation
and observability stack.

299
00:20:43,572 --> 00:20:46,992
Now let's make this more concrete.

300
00:20:47,812 --> 00:20:51,172
What does a mature production
evaluation architecture look like?

301
00:20:51,702 --> 00:20:54,962
I see it is a combination of
evaluation pipelines, observability

302
00:20:55,772 --> 00:20:59,802
tooling, interaction tracing,
and governance control.

303
00:21:00,582 --> 00:21:03,702
First, we need a real-time dashboard.

304
00:21:04,282 --> 00:21:09,292
We want to see things like
hallucination rate, latency, refusal

305
00:21:09,292 --> 00:21:14,172
rates, user satisfaction, task
success, and evaluation scores.

306
00:21:14,692 --> 00:21:18,252
And we don't just want one overall number.

307
00:21:18,902 --> 00:21:20,972
We need to see performance by segment.

308
00:21:21,892 --> 00:21:24,302
So the second capability
is the segment performance.

309
00:21:24,702 --> 00:21:29,362
For example, we might analyze
results by model version, prompt

310
00:21:29,412 --> 00:21:34,522
template, user segment, use case,
or a deployment environment.

311
00:21:35,072 --> 00:21:37,802
This is critical for
a root cause analysis.

312
00:21:38,432 --> 00:21:41,772
Imagine overall quality
drops by five percent.

313
00:21:42,402 --> 00:21:46,022
That's a useful information,
but it doesn't tell us why.

314
00:21:47,282 --> 00:21:51,822
But suppose we discover that the
degradation is isolated to one model

315
00:21:52,022 --> 00:21:58,502
version or one prompt ver-- template,
now we have got some actionable insights.

316
00:22:00,122 --> 00:22:03,391
And the third capability is
the full interaction tracing.

317
00:22:03,961 --> 00:22:08,812
We want to able to construct the
complete chain input, retrieved

318
00:22:08,812 --> 00:22:14,022
context, model output, evaluation
score, and associated metadata.

319
00:22:14,671 --> 00:22:15,512
That gives us the reproducibility.

320
00:22:16,512 --> 00:22:20,902
It gives us the observability,
and most importantly, it gives

321
00:22:20,932 --> 00:22:25,182
engineers the ability to investigate
the failures rather than simply

322
00:22:25,182 --> 00:22:26,822
seeing that something went wrong

323
00:22:27,822 --> 00:22:30,652
Next comes the important
thing, governance.

324
00:22:30,972 --> 00:22:32,912
Build trust into your engineer.

325
00:22:33,892 --> 00:22:38,642
Now, none of this work at
scale without governance.

326
00:22:39,632 --> 00:22:41,892
And I want to emphasize one point here.

327
00:22:42,222 --> 00:22:44,882
Governance should not be treated
as a compli-compliance checkbox.

328
00:22:46,662 --> 00:22:49,812
It should be embedded directly
into engineering process.

329
00:22:50,232 --> 00:22:54,572
That means things like
model versioning, evaluation

330
00:22:55,572 --> 00:22:57,982
snapshots, automated rollback triggers
human escalation SLAs, audit logs,

331
00:22:58,272 --> 00:23:03,152
evaluation gates, and documented
compliance controls and methodologies.

332
00:23:04,702 --> 00:23:05,932
Why is this important?

333
00:23:06,202 --> 00:23:10,102
Because when something goes wrong,
we need to know what version of

334
00:23:10,112 --> 00:23:11,702
the system produced the result.

335
00:23:12,472 --> 00:23:14,142
Which model was being used?

336
00:23:15,022 --> 00:23:18,502
Which evaluation snapshot did we validate?

337
00:23:19,632 --> 00:23:21,022
Which prompt version?

338
00:23:21,562 --> 00:23:22,812
What configuration?

339
00:23:23,302 --> 00:23:24,792
What happened in production?

340
00:23:25,272 --> 00:23:29,542
Without that traceability,
debugging becomes much harder and

341
00:23:30,002 --> 00:23:31,292
accountability becomes weaker.

342
00:23:32,172 --> 00:23:37,722
Governance built into engineering
gives us a durable trust.

343
00:23:39,272 --> 00:23:43,462
Governance added after the fact
tends to become a paperwork.

344
00:23:43,462 --> 00:23:47,802
So objective is not simply
to document what happened.

345
00:23:49,042 --> 00:23:54,842
The objective is to engineer the system
so that the right controls exist by design

346
00:23:55,842 --> 00:23:58,562
Connect AI metrics to
the business outcomes.

347
00:23:59,062 --> 00:24:04,602
This is probably the most important slide
in the presentation, because technical

348
00:24:04,642 --> 00:24:09,072
metrics are useful only when they
help us understand the business value.

349
00:24:10,172 --> 00:24:15,172
It's very easy for an engineering
team to optimize a technical number.

350
00:24:15,672 --> 00:24:21,902
We improve latency, we improve evaluation
score, we reduce hallucination rate.

351
00:24:22,462 --> 00:24:26,962
All of those can be valuable, but
leadership ultimately needs to

352
00:24:26,962 --> 00:24:31,442
understand what does the improvement
actually do for the business?

353
00:24:32,392 --> 00:24:37,762
Do we need to-- So we need to
collect connect them to the metrics.

354
00:24:38,442 --> 00:24:40,622
Take a customer support as an example.

355
00:24:41,002 --> 00:24:45,662
Higher task success should
lead to higher resolution rate.

356
00:24:46,072 --> 00:24:49,052
Higher resolution rate
should reduce escalation.

357
00:24:49,632 --> 00:24:52,732
A fewer escalation should
reduce operational costs.

358
00:24:53,262 --> 00:24:55,112
Now consider content generation.

359
00:24:55,612 --> 00:25:00,632
Improving AI quality should lead
to higher editorial ex-acceptance,

360
00:25:01,152 --> 00:25:06,511
and higher acceptance can translate
into greater team productivity.

361
00:25:06,832 --> 00:25:08,732
Or consider decision support.

362
00:25:09,511 --> 00:25:14,352
Better evidence quality should
produce better recommendations,

363
00:25:14,551 --> 00:25:18,611
and a better recommendation should
pro-improve downstream decision.

364
00:25:19,152 --> 00:25:24,462
So the important idea is that technical
quality should have a path to an outcome.

365
00:25:26,162 --> 00:25:30,362
This creates a common language
across engineering, product,

366
00:25:30,502 --> 00:25:32,072
operations, and leadership.

367
00:25:32,581 --> 00:25:38,582
Instead of saying our evaluation score
improved by three points, we can say

368
00:25:38,632 --> 00:25:45,532
our system is resolving more requests
successfully, reducing escalation

369
00:25:45,592 --> 00:25:48,231
and improving the user outcome.

370
00:25:48,831 --> 00:25:54,842
That's much stronger way to communicate
the value of AI engineering So

371
00:25:54,852 --> 00:25:59,262
let's move on to the what are
the key take-- key takeaways.

372
00:25:59,602 --> 00:26:03,622
So let me close with
the four key takeaways.

373
00:26:03,662 --> 00:26:05,852
First, go beyond benchmarks.

374
00:26:06,602 --> 00:26:12,522
Benchmarks are useful, but static scores
do not guarantee production reliability.

375
00:26:13,572 --> 00:26:15,152
Evaluate the complete system.

376
00:26:15,882 --> 00:26:18,452
Second, measure what matters.

377
00:26:19,592 --> 00:26:25,912
Track factuality, ground-
grounding, safety, task

378
00:26:26,092 --> 00:26:29,092
suc-success, and real user outcome.

379
00:26:30,012 --> 00:26:33,722
Third, combine automation
with human judgment.

380
00:26:34,161 --> 00:26:39,831
Use automation for scale and use
expert validation when-- where the,

381
00:26:40,831 --> 00:26:44,171
the scenarios are complex or high risk.

382
00:26:44,741 --> 00:26:48,151
Fourth, evaluate continuously
and govern by design.

383
00:26:49,341 --> 00:26:54,251
Evaluation, monitoring, traceability,
and accountability should be treated

384
00:26:54,271 --> 00:26:56,311
as a core engineering disciplines.

385
00:26:56,781 --> 00:27:02,151
And that leads to the final idea
wh-- I want to leave you with.

386
00:27:02,601 --> 00:27:07,321
The ultimate measure of an AI
system is not simply how well the

387
00:27:07,331 --> 00:27:09,321
model performs on a benchmark.

388
00:27:09,951 --> 00:27:16,032
The ultimate measure is whether
the system can consistently deliver

389
00:27:16,451 --> 00:27:19,961
reliable, governed, measurable
outcomes into production.

390
00:27:21,391 --> 00:27:25,521
That stand-- That's the stand
we should be aiming for.

391
00:27:26,851 --> 00:27:32,021
Not just impressive models, not
just impressive benchmark numbers,

392
00:27:32,821 --> 00:27:37,691
but a AI system that people can
actually trust and depend upon

393
00:27:38,691 --> 00:27:40,501
Thank you everyone for your time.

394
00:27:40,841 --> 00:27:46,171
I hope this discussion has provided
a useful perspective on how we

395
00:27:46,221 --> 00:27:52,391
think about an evaluation as
Gen, GenAI moves into production.

396
00:27:52,761 --> 00:27:58,751
The opportunity with Ge- GenAI
is e-enormous, but realizing

397
00:27:58,761 --> 00:28:03,071
that opportunity requires us to
think beyond the mo-model itself.

398
00:28:03,881 --> 00:28:09,861
We need evaluation, we need observability,
we need continuous feedback, we need

399
00:28:11,131 --> 00:28:17,291
governance, and we need to connect all
of those technical capabilities back

400
00:28:17,291 --> 00:28:21,131
to the user and business outcomes.

401
00:28:22,331 --> 00:28:22,821
Thank you.

